Third term: √6. So adding all three terms:

√6/3 + 8√6/3 + √6. Yes, converting √6 to 3√6/3. So total is (1 + 8 + 3)√6 /3? Wait, no. Wait, the first term is √6/3, second term is 8√6/3, third term is 3√6/3. Then adding the numerators: 1 + 8 + 3 = 12. So 12√6/3 = 4√6. Correct. Then divide by √6. So 4√6 / √6 = 4. Then square root of 4 is 2. So yes, the simplified expression is 2. That seems right. Let me check with another approach.

Alternatively, maybe I can simplify each term before adding. Let me see.

Original expression:

sqrt[ ( (4 / sqrt(24)) + (sqrt(3456)/9) + sqrt(6) ) / sqrt(6) ]

Alternatively, notice that dividing by sqrt(6) is the same as multiplying by 1/sqrt(6). So maybe I can combine each term with 1/sqrt(6).

Let me rewrite the entire numerator divided by sqrt(6):

[ (4 / sqrt(24)) + (sqrt(3456)/9) + sqrt(6) ] / sqrt(6) = (4 / sqrt(24)) / sqrt(6) + (sqrt(3456)/9)/sqrt(6) + sqrt(6)/sqrt(6)

