If I consider the standard basis vectors, the projection of the vector i = (1, 0) onto the y-axis is (0, 0). Because the x-component is eliminated. The projection of the vector j = (0, 1) onto the y-axis is (0, 1), since it's already on the y-axis. 

So, the projection matrix P should satisfy P*i = 0 and P*j = j. Therefore, the columns of the projection matrix are the projections of the standard basis vectors. 

So, the first column of P is P*e1, which is the projection of e1 (which is (1,0)) onto the y-axis, which is (0,0). The second column is P*e2, which is the projection of e2 (which is (0,1)) onto the y-axis, which is (0,1). Therefore, the matrix P would be:

[0 0]
[0 1]

